# Cyrius Dependency Management — Vidya Reference
# How cyrius.toml deps work, stdlib vs project deps, and the dep resolution flow.
# 2026-04-15; fold table re-read 2026-10-06 at the cyrius 6.6.20 closeout — ALL TWELVE moved at
# 6.6.19 (the 12-fold wave) — again 2026-10-21 at cyrius 6.7.16 (sigil 3.13.13, patra 1.16.6,
# yukti 2.3.18, sandhi 1.13.3), and at cyrius 6.7.17 (bayan 1.11.0, sandhi 1.13.6, sankoch 2.8.9). This file holds the ONE vidya copy of the table; core / index /
# ecosystem / stdlib_format point here instead of carrying their own.

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_toml_overview"
description = "cyrius.cyml is the project manifest — declares build config, stdlib deps, and external deps (legacy cyrius.toml is a fallback)"
category = "cyrius"
---
Every Cyrius project has a cyrius.cyml at the root (CYML = TOML header +
markdown body, v5.0.0+). Legacy cyrius.toml is still read as a fallback.
Three sections matter for deps:

    [package]
    name = "myproject"
    version = "0.1.0"

    [deps]
    stdlib = ["string", "fmt", "alloc", "io", "vec", "str", "syscalls", "assert"]

    [deps.sakshi]
    git = "https://github.com/MacCracken/sakshi.git"
    tag = "2.2.3"
    modules = ["dist/sakshi.cyr"]

The [deps] section has two forms:
1. stdlib = [...] — copies modules from the installed Cyrius stdlib into lib/
2. [deps.NAME] — clones a git repo at a tag and copies specific module files

Running `cyrius deps` resolves both. All resolved files land in lib/ as
absolute symlinks (falls back to copy). The compiler auto-includes them
via the dep include list. Namespaced deps map to lib/{depname}_{basename};
if the basename matches depname, lib/{depname}.cyr alias is also created.

[[entries]]
name = "deps_lever1_granularity"
description = "dependency-model lever 1 (Phases A-D): requires key, .deps sidecar, [groups], distlib --modular per-module extraction, and the v6.3.32 built-in std default group that dissolves the stdlib category"
category = "cyrius"
---
v6.2.46–.50 added "lever 1" of the dependency model — declaring + resolving
deps at module granularity instead of hand-ordering whole folds. Five pieces:

1. requires (v6.2.46) — a named dep declares the stdlib leaves it needs:

    [deps.sigil]
    git = "..."
    tag = "3.9.5"
    requires = ["ct", "keccak", "random"]

   `cyrius deps` pulls each leaf through the recursive stdlib resolver AHEAD
   of the dep's own modules (topological by resolution order), dedups, and
   FAILS LOUD on a missing leaf — replacing the old "ct must precede sigil"
   hand-ordering + its omit-one → runtime ud2/SIGILL trap.

2. .deps sidecar (v6.2.47/.48) — `cyrius distlib` emits dist/<pkg>.deps next
   to the fold, listing the lib/ leaves it strips at fold time (the fold
   destroys its own includes). `cyrius deps` auto-reads it, so a consumer
   declaring just [deps.<pkg>] gets the fold's stdlib in scope — no requires
   needed. The producer scans its umbrella include-hub (src/lib.cyr /
   src/main.cyr) AND excludes its own [deps.NAME] folds (those resolve
   transitively — listing them would double-include). As of v6.4.48,
   `cyrius distlib <profile>` SUBSETS the per-profile `.deps` sidecar: a
   `[lib.<profile>]` fold's `.deps` now lists only its OWN referenced
   stdlib leaves, not the whole bundle's chain (e.g. a `sha` profile no
   longer drags the full crypto suite's deps).

   v6.5.10 — the BASE bundle's sidecar is now the manifest's declared
   `[deps] stdlib` UNIONED with the include-scan, not the scan alone. The
   scan only ever saw literal `include "lib/X.cyr"` lines, so it captured
   what a module INCLUDES and never what it REFERENCES through the
   declaration (setu emitted 8 leaves against a declared 12; `result`,
   `net`, `chrono`, `args` were all referenced at top level and missed).
   That matters beyond documentation: `cyrius deps` VALIDATES a consumer's
   `[deps] stdlib` against the dep's sidecar, so an under-reporting sidecar
   silently switched OFF that check — the same under-declaring consumer
   built `OK` against the 8-leaf sidecar and hard-errored (naming `chrono`
   and `args`) against the corrected 12-leaf one. ⚠ BASE BUNDLE ONLY: a
   profile is a narrow subset of modules, so unioning the whole declaration
   into it would OVER-report and fail a legitimately-narrow consumer.
   Profiles keep the pruned inference of v6.4.48.

   v6.5.8 — `cyrius distlib --all` regenerates every `[lib.X]` profile in
   one invocation and `--check` compares BYTES (not version strings) to
   report a stale profile. Before this, distlib did exactly one bundle per
   run and nothing could ask the manifest which profiles exist — the N+1
   ritual that nearly shipped sankoch 2.7.6's gzip fix with all nine
   sub-bundles still carrying the buggy encoder under a fresh version
   string.

3. [groups] (v6.2.49) — name a recurring set once, expand at resolve time:

    [groups]
    crypto = ["ct", "keccak", "random"]

   Referenced in [deps].stdlib or a [deps.NAME] requires. Nested (groups of
   groups), deduped, self/mutual cycles skipped. Byte-identical when absent.

4. distlib --modular (v6.2.50) — per-module extraction. Emits
   dist/<pkg>/<module>.cyr (one file per [lib].modules entry, src/ self-refs
   stripped, lib/ kept) + dist/<pkg>/index.cyml — a [modular] dep graph
   `<module> = [siblings…, "lib:<leaf>"…]`. The flat single-file distlib is
   unchanged (default).

5. modular = [...] (v6.2.50) — a consumer pulls EXACT sub-modules:

    [deps.sigil]
    path = "../sigil"
    modular = ["ed25519"]

   The resolver reads dist/sigil/index.cyml and pulls ed25519 + only its
   transitive siblings + lib leaves (topo order) into lib/sigil_ed25519.cyr —
   NOT the whole monolith.

6. std default group (v6.3.32 Phase D — lever-1 COMPLETION, dissolves the "stdlib"
   category). `std` is a BUILT-IN [groups]-style group naming the universal core
   leaves: ["string", "fmt", "alloc", "io", "vec", "str", "syscalls"] — the set
   every real program pulls (matches the `cyrius init` templates). Reference it in
   the stdlib list instead of hand-ordering the core:

    [deps]
    stdlib = ["std", "chrono", "net"]   # std expands to the 7 universals

   `std` expands via the same [groups] path (item 3), in the SAME order as the
   explicit list, so a `["std", …]` list resolves the SAME leaves as spelling the
   7 out — byte-identical, and it drops the omit-one→SIGILL order-sensitivity the
   old hand-ordered list had. A manifest that defines its OWN `[groups] std = [...]`
   OVERRIDES the built-in (registered after the manifest's [groups] parse). Manifests
   that never name `std` are unaffected. `stdlib` remains the section key (back-compat
   alias); the flagship migration is descent (18-element hand list → `["std", <9
   non-core>]`, same symbol set). This completes lever 1 (Phases A–D).

SECURITY (v6.2.51, the include filename-capture overflow bug): sub-module / leaf / pkg names from a dep-controlled
index.cyml are validated (reject any / or ..) before any filesystem op — a
malicious index sibling like `a/../../../x` would otherwise delete an arbitrary
.cyr during automatic `cyrius deps`.

Lever 2 (v6.3.1) builds on [groups] + --modular: required-vs-optional deps,
features, and platform/target scoping. The undefined-fn reachable-call
hard-error (safe once optional refs are declarable) follows in its own slot
v6.3.2 (un-bundled once lever 2 landed + its 21/192 corpus blast radius was
measured).

[[entries]]
name = "deps_lever2_optional"
description = "v6.3.1 dependency-model lever 2: optional = true, the [features] table, --features/--no-default-features, and platform-conditional target= keys (axes combine)"
category = "cyrius"
---
v6.3.1 added "lever 2" — restricting WHICH build contexts pull a dep, on top
of lever 1's WHAT-granularity. Three axes that combine:

1. optional = true — a dep that resolves only when an active feature names it:

    [deps.wgpu]
    git = "..."
    optional = true

   With no feature naming it, it is never fetched. A plain required dep (no
   `optional`) always resolves, exactly as before.

2. [features] table (Cargo-style) — named sets that activate optional deps:

    [features]
    default = ["std-io"]
    gpu = ["wgpu"]
    win-shell = ["mabda"]

   `cyrius build --features gpu` activates wgpu; plain `cyrius build` activates
   only `default` (unless --no-default-features). A feature's members may name
   other features (recursively expanded, cycle-guarded) or optional deps; a
   `--features wgpu` naming an optional dep directly activates it too.

3. target = "<arch|os>" — resolves only on a matching build target:

    [deps.mabda]
    optional = true
    target = "windows"

   Arch axis: x86_64 / x86 / aarch64 / arm64 (vs the build's --aarch64). OS
   axis: windows / agnos / bare-metal (vs --win/--agnos/--target=*-bare-metal),
   and "linux" = a native userland build. An unrecognized string never matches.

The axes COMBINE — a dep gated out on EITHER (inactive feature OR mismatched
target) is skipped entirely: no clone, no requires-pull, no module copy, no
include push. A skipped dep is NOT marked visited, so a later transitive
*required* reference can still resolve it.

GOTCHA (the ordering hazard): `cyrius`'s dep resolver (_auto_deps) fires BEFORE
each subcommand's own flag loop, so --features/--no-default-features and the
target flags are parsed in a PRE-SCAN ahead of resolution (re-reading them in
the subcommand loop is idempotent). Wiring them only into the build loop would
run the resolver with stale state.

100% cbt-side (cbt/deps.cyr + cbt/core.cyr + cbt/cyrius.cyr) — touches no src/,
so cycc is byte-identical. A manifest with no [features]/optional/target
resolves the identical include set + order as v6.2.x. Feature unification
across transitive deps stays out of scope (the consumer manifest's [features]
drives the process-global active set).

[[entries]]
name = "stdlib_deps"
description = "stdlib deps copy .cyr files from the installed Cyrius distribution into the project lib/"
category = "cyrius"
---
stdlib = ["string", "fmt", "alloc", "io", "vec", "str", "syscalls", "assert"]

Each name maps to lib/{name}.cyr in the Cyrius installation. `cyrius deps` copies
these files into the project's lib/ directory. The compiler then auto-includes them.

Common stdlib modules and what they provide:

  string   — strlen, streq, memeq, memcpy, memset, memchr, strchr, atoi, strstr
  fmt      — fmt_int, fmt_hex, fmt_sprintf (with bufsz), fmt_printf, fmt_float
  alloc    — bump allocator (alloc_init, alloc, alloc_reset), arena allocator
             (v6.5.9: arenas take an exhaustion POLICY — ARENA_FULL_NULL (0,
             the default, unchanged behaviour) / _GROW (1, chains another
             chunk) / _SPILL (2, overflow from the global allocator) /
             _ABORT (3, die at the allocation). ⚠ GROW keeps its chunks
             across arena_reset — the bump allocator underneath has no
             free() — so a request loop converges on its high-water mark.
             v6.5.7: alloc_reset() INVALIDATES every pointer it handed out,
             which used to include the memoized default allocator's own
             vtable; that now lives outside any resettable region.
             v6.5.10: alloc_via reads the vtable inline — 15.1 → 11 ns.)
  io       — file_read_all, file_write_all, file_exists, dir_list
  vec      — dynamic array (vec_new, vec_push, vec_get, vec_pop, vec_find,
             vec_sort_by / vec_select_nth since v6.5.4)
  str      — heap-allocated strings (str_from, str_cat, str_split, str_builder)
  syscalls — Linux syscall wrappers (open, close, read, write, fork, exec, etc.)
  assert   — test assertions (assert_eq, assert, test_group, assert_summary)
  net      — TCP sockets (tcp_socket, sockaddr_in)
  thread   — threads + channels (thread_create, thread_join, chan_send;
             thread_create_detached + thread_is_done since v6.5.8 — an
             unjoined thread used to leak its whole 2 MiB stack, and
             thread_join had a lost-wakeup deadlock, ~1 join in 150k).
             ⚠ thread_is_done is valid only BEFORE thread_join — join
             consumes the handle.
  sync     — mutex / condvar (mutex_lock; peers sync_macos.cyr,
             sync_windows.cyr). The Linux mutex went three-state at
             v6.5.9: 392 → 48 ns per uncontended lock/unlock pair,
             because a 2-state futex cell cannot tell the releaser
             whether anyone is parked, so it woke the kernel on EVERY
             release.
  hashmap  — string-keyed hash table (map_new, map_set, map_get, map_has)
  bayan    — JSON / TOML / CYML / CSV / base64 / bigint / u128
             (carved from stdlib at v6.1.25; include "lib/bayan.cyr")
  tls      — TLS 1.2 / 1.3 client + server: native over sigil by default,
             libssl.so.3 via fdlopen opt-out (-D CYRIUS_TLS_LIBSSL)

IMPORTANT: alloc_init() must be called before any alloc() calls. Most stdlib
modules that allocate (vec, str, hashmap, bayan) require it.

[[entries]]
name = "project_deps"
description = "Project deps clone a git repo at a pinned tag and copy specific module files into lib/"
category = "cyrius"
---
External dependencies are declared as [deps.NAME] sections in cyrius.toml:

    [deps.sigil]
    git = "https://github.com/MacCracken/sigil.git"
    tag = "3.0.1"
    modules = ["dist/sigil.cyr"]

Fields:
  git     — repository URL (validated: shell metacharacters rejected)
  tag     — git tag to clone (pinned version — no floating branches)
  modules — list of .cyr files relative to repo root to import
  path    — (optional) local path instead of git clone

Resolution flow:
1. `cyrius deps` reads the [deps.NAME] section
2. Clones to $CYRIUS_HOME/deps/{name}/{tag}/ (shallow, cached)
3. For each module in modules[]:
   - Source: {clone_dir}/{module_path}
   - Dest:   lib/{depname}_{basename}  (always namespaced)
   - Creates absolute symlink (falls back to copy if symlink fails)
4. If basename matches depname (e.g. sigil.cyr for [deps.sigil]),
   also creates lib/{depname}.cyr alias for canonical include path
5. Adds to auto-include list

Consumers include via: include "lib/sigil.cyr"
The namespaced form (lib/sigil_sigil.cyr) also works but the alias is preferred.

[[entries]]
name = "dep_bundle_pattern"
description = "Multi-file deps must bundle into a single dist/*.cyr file for distribution"
category = "cyrius"
---
Cyrius has no multi-file module system — the compiler processes a single compilation
unit (one file, with includes expanded). External deps with multiple source files
must bundle them into a single .cyr file.

The standard pattern (used by sigil, patra, yukti, mabda, sankoch):

1. Create scripts/bundle.sh that concatenates sources in dependency order
2. Strip include lines (grep -v "^include ")
3. Add a header with version, source URL, license, usage
4. Output to dist/{name}.cyr

Example bundle.sh:
    for f in src/types.cyr src/core.cyr src/api.cyr; do
        echo "# --- $(basename "$f") ---"
        grep -v "^include " "$ROOT/$f"
    done > "$ROOT/dist/mylib.cyr"

Then cyrius.toml references: modules = ["dist/mylib.cyr"]

Common gotchas when bundling:
- Duplicate variable names across files: Cyrius has no block scoping for
  if/elif, so `var x` in one elif and `var x` in another elif within the
  same function collide. Hoist to function top and reassign.
- Reserved words as variable names: `match`, `type`, `break` etc. Rename.
- Large static arrays (var buf[262144]): will overflow the 1MB output buffer.
  Heap-allocate with alloc() instead.
- File ordering: types/constants first, then low-level, then high-level, then
  public API last.

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_update_flow"
description = "cyrius update refreshes lib/ and .cyrius-toolchain from the installed Cyrius version"
category = "cyrius"
---
`cyrius update` does two things:

1. Copies all .cyr files from the installed Cyrius stdlib into lib/
   (reads $CYRIUS_HOME/current to find the installed version)
2. Updates .cyrius-toolchain to match the installed version

This is the primary upgrade mechanism. When a new Cyrius version is installed:
  $ cyrius update

After updating, re-run `cyrius deps` to refresh external deps if needed.

The .cyrius-toolchain file pins the minimum Cyrius version for CI reproducibility.
`cyrius init` creates it from VERSION; `cyrius update` keeps it current.

As of v5.0.0, the canonical manifest is cyrius.cyml (CYML = TOML header +
markdown body); legacy cyrius.toml is still read as a fallback.

[[entries]]
name = "existing_deps_registry"
description = "Current Cyrius ecosystem deps: 12 sovereign stdlib libraries, all folded byte-identical into lib/ (0 git deps); fold versions re-read from the lib/ headers at cyrius 6.7.17 (bayan 1.11.0, sandhi 1.13.6 and sankoch 2.8.9 refolded in 6.7.17's wave 0; sigil 3.13.13, patra 1.16.6 and yukti 2.3.18 in 6.7.16's wave 0, sandhi 1.13.3 at its close; patra 1.16.5 in 6.7.15; sakshi 2.5.10 in 6.7.11; the other five at their 6.7.6 / 6.7.10 refolds); since 6.7.16 no cyrius gate reads a fold repo's checkout"
category = "cyrius"
---
Since v6.5.10 the ecosystem libraries are folded byte-identical into
the stdlib (0 git deps). CURRENT FOLDS at cyrius 6.7.17 — each read from
the header of the vendored lib/<name>.cyr and checked against cyrius
`docs/ecosystem.md`'s rows, not carried from this list (every row below
had gone stale since v6.5.10 until it was re-read at 6.6.15, and ALL
TWELVE had drifted again by 6.7.11 — this table still read the 6.6.19
wave):

  sigil    3.13.13    patra    1.16.6     sankoch  2.8.9
  sakshi   2.5.10     bayan    1.11.0     ganita   1.2.15
  mabda    4.2.0      niyama   1.1.0      yantra   1.0.11
  vani     1.2.10     sandhi   1.13.6     yukti    2.3.18

Refolded in 6.7.17's wave 0, on main, each byte-identical to the user's tag of
2026-10-22 (pins cyrius 6.7.16): bayan 1.9.0 -> 1.11.0 (tag @ 6479739; 3540e8535 —
1.10.0: the 99 public constants are `const`, the 32 public predicates return `: bool`;
1.11.0: `bayan_toml_v_parse` parses TOML 1.0.0 strictly into json's value tree, its
bare-name lookups are deprecated for the typed `_by_str` names, which cyrius's own callers —
the `#derive(Deserialize)` expansion among them — moved to in the same bite), sandhi 1.13.3 ->
1.13.6 (tag @ d5576bc; c192a19d9 — the HPACK tables built once under concurrent h2 starts,
the TLS session cache under a mutex, HTTP/2 flow control) and sankoch 2.8.7 -> 2.8.9 (tag
@ 21470ae; 442dbfada — batch inflate / deflate keep no per-call 32 / 40 B).

Refolded in 6.7.16, each byte-identical to the user's tag, each a patch
with no public fn added or removed: in wave 0 on main, sigil 3.13.12 ->
3.13.13 (tag @ 2ed419c; b382b7324), patra 1.16.5 -> 1.16.6 (tag @
efa2e34; 48d3fe294, with D34 extended to `patra_bind_text` before the
tag, 5e45b3063) and yukti 2.3.17 -> 2.3.18 (tag @ 3efbba9; 87190c421, a
fold defect the tools lane found), their pins unchanged; at the close,
sandhi 1.13.1 -> 1.13.3 (tag @ cd5d541; 571f1a600 — 1.13.2 never folded
alone; pin 6.7.12 -> 6.7.15). The per-dep notes below say what each
changed. Refolded
in 6.7.15: patra 1.16.4 -> 1.16.5 (tag @ 9c67b33, the user's
tag; NOT tests only this time — the refold changes CODE in
lib/patra.cyr: `_bytes_read_chain`, `_rs_materialize`,
`patra_result_read_bytes` and `patra_result_read_text`, from three 6.7.15
lanes: the platform lane's suite fixtures, the libfix lane's `fuzz_bytes`
fix and the read path it exposed, libfix3's oversized ref; no public fn
added or removed, pin unchanged). Refolded in 6.7.14: patra 1.16.3 ->
1.16.4 (tag @ bbded00; tests only —
`dist/patra.cyr` differs in its `# Version:` line alone). Refolded in
6.7.13: patra 1.16.2 -> 1.16.3 (tag @ b8722b7), bayan 1.6.0 ->
1.9.0 (tag @ 0c626c6), sandhi 1.10.9 -> 1.13.1 (tag @ bc7ef13). Refolded
in 6.7.12: bayan 1.5.14 -> 1.6.0 (tag @ dd8f9c7). Refolded in
6.7.11: sakshi 2.5.10, sankoch 2.8.4 -> 2.8.7 (2.8.6 by the
stdlib lane, 2.8.7 at the integration), patra 1.16.2. 6.7.10: sigil
3.13.12, yukti 2.3.17, bayan 1.5.14, yantra 1.0.11 (sakshi 2.5.9 then).
6.7.6 (the W2 wave): sandhi 1.10.9, vani 1.2.10, niyama 1.1.0, mabda
4.2.0, ganita 1.2.15 (and patra 1.16.0, sankoch 2.8.3 then).

Re-derive (all twelve use `# Version:` since 6.6.19; the loop still
matches the pre-6.6.19 sakshi shape, for reading an older tree — see
below):

    for n in sakshi bayan sandhi sigil ganita niyama mabda vani yantra yukti patra sankoch; do
      grep -m1 -oE '(# Version:|Bundled distribution of [a-z]+) *v?[0-9.]+' lib/$n.cyr; done

cyrius's own copy, `docs/ecosystem.md`, is GATED against lib/ by
`tests/gates/toolchain/fold_table_matches_vendored.sh`; this one is not,
which is why it is kept in ONE place.

⛔ THE FOLD GATES READ ONLY THE CYRIUS TREE (since 6.7.16, W0-1 — the
user, 2026-10-19: "LANGUAGES DO NOT GATE ON OTHER PROJECTS"). Two gates
had read a fold repo's checkout: folds_agnos_parity.sh compared its
FOLD_DEPS row with `$HOME/Repos/<fold>/cyrius.cyml` when that checkout sat
at the folded version — that axis left, and the in-tree axes stay (each
fold built ALONE for Linux and agnos against its declared closure, the
borrow detector, the include walk, both anti-vacuous rows, the 10-of-12
floor); distlib_named_deps_anchored.sh axis 4 counted the leaves of
`$HOME/Repos/patra/dist/patra.deps` — it now builds a fixture with this
tree's CLI and checks the PLAIN `cyrius distlib` sidecar it writes.
removed_symbol_census.sh reads this checkout only (it walked ~146 sibling
repos). tests/gates/toolchain/gates_read_only_the_tree.sh refuses a
`$HOME/Repos` read in any spelling across tests/gates, scripts/*.sh and
programs/checks — an allowlist of zero. A fold's byte-identity to its
source (`sha256sum lib/<n>.cyr ~/Repos/<n>/dist/<n>.cyr`) is checked BY
HAND at the refold; the fold's own suite is gated by its own isolated CI.

(6.6.19: all twelve re-vendored byte-identical from the tags the
post-6.6.18 wave released, and since then every fold carries its
compile-verified requires block — language/tooling.cyml
`distlib_sidecar_is_what_the_compile_verify_proves_v6618`. 6.6.16:
ganita 1.2.11 -> 1.2.13 and sandhi 1.10.4 -> 1.10.7, the intermediate
tags never folded on their own.)

(sigil 3.12.15 + patra 1.12.12: thread-local slots migrated onto cyrius's
thread_local_alloc allocator, v6.4.65. sankoch 2.7.6: the gzip/deflate
block-boundary corruption fix, v6.4.79 — fixed UPSTREAM first, then folded.
sakshi 2.4.8 + bayan 1.4.1: both folded at cyrius v6.5.8 — sakshi for the
`_sk_fmt_int` half of the `i64::MIN` formatter class, bayan for a
`json_v_obj_get` cstring-vs-`Str` key mismatch. Both fixed upstream first.)

HOW TO VERIFY A FOLD VERSION — read the header of `lib/<name>.cyr`. Since
6.6.19's re-vendor (sakshi 2.5.7) all twelve folds use ONE shape:

    lib/sigil.cyr:   # sigil.cyr -- bundled distribution
                     # Version: 3.13.13
    lib/sakshi.cyr:  # sakshi.cyr -- bundled distribution
                     # Version: 2.5.10

History: up to sakshi 2.5.6 its fold used a SECOND shape,
`# Bundled distribution of sakshi v2.4.8`, and a `# Version:`-only grep
silently skipped it — that is exactly how a previous sweep left the base
logger un-bumped while reporting the fold table clean. When reading a
pre-6.6.19 tree, match both shapes, or read the file.

TWO MORE FOLD TRAPS worth knowing before you touch a vendored lib:
  - `cyrius distlib` regenerates ONLY the main bundle. Every `[lib.<profile>]`
    sub-profile keeps the OLD code until regenerated too — sankoch 2.7.6
    shipped with all 9 sub-profiles still carrying the 2.7.5 bug.
  - `cyrius lib sync` would REVERT every fold (it now refuses, v6.4.77).

The headline version on each per-dep note below is the LIVE fold (6.7.17);
the prose under it is accumulated lineage and still names the release
that introduced each change — what each fold changed since is in cyrius
`docs/ecosystem.md`'s row for it and in that sibling's own CHANGELOG.

NOTE on transitive deps — the blanket "DIRECT deps only, hand-list all
transitives" that used to sit here was superseded by lever 1: a dep's
`requires` key (v6.2.46) and the `dist/<pkg>.deps` sidecar (v6.2.47/.48,
auto-read by `_dep_read_sidecar` in cbt/deps.cyr) both pull a dep's own
stdlib leaves ahead of its modules, and `modular =` (v6.2.50) walks
`index.cyml` for transitive siblings. See the deps_lever1_granularity
entry. What is still absent is a LOCKFILE and closest-wins version
resolution — moot at the moment (0 git deps; everything is folded), and
it re-matters the day a git dep comes back.


  sakshi (2.5.10) — Tracing and error handling. Two profiles:
                     sakshi.cyr (stderr-only, minimal) and
                     sakshi_full.cyr (ring buffer, UDP, span stack).
                     Zero heap allocation. Zero external deps.
                     2.5.10 (folded at cyrius 6.7.11, tag @ 569e9f8):
                     four private buffers declared in BYTES, not slots
                     (`_sk_ring_buf` / `_sk_aring_buf: u8[4096]`,
                     `_sk_udp_addr: u8[16]`, `_sk_span_stack: u8[384]` —
                     a bare top-level `var x[N]` is N slots, 8N bytes):
                     static data 69,232 -> 9,088 B, so including sandhi
                     or yantra no longer prints the large-static-data
                     advisory (the advisory named sandhi; the cause was
                     sakshi). No API or behaviour change. 2.5.9 (6.7.10):
                     compiles and runs on cx.

  patra (1.16.6) — Structured storage and SQL queries. 1.16.6 (folded at
                     cyrius 6.7.16, tag @ efa2e34): A NEGATIVE BYTES /
                     TEXT LENGTH IS REFUSED AT THE WRITE —
                     `patra_insert_row` / `patra_insert_row_or_ignore`
                     answer `PATRA_ERR_PARAM` (11) and write no row (they
                     answered `PATRA_OK` and stored the length raw), and
                     `patra_bind_text` answers `PATRA_ERR_PARAM` and
                     stores nothing, the slot keeping what it held (an
                     INSERT / UPDATE had written the length — a TEXT cell
                     kept it, a STR cell was emptied — and a WHERE value
                     matched nothing) (the user's D34, extended to the
                     bind at the fold wave). A negative length already
                     stored — by an older patra or a damaged file —
                     reads length -1 from the getters and
                     `PATRA_ERR_PAGE` from the reads, as a ref longer
                     than the file does (1.16.5's getters answered the
                     raw value; D35). The three fixes are
                     `_patra_insert_row_impl`, `_rs_materialize` and
                     `patra_bind_text` (50 + / 26 - against 1.16.5's
                     fold). Suite 2,075 / 2,075 (1.16.5: 1,752), also
                     under qemu-aarch64; isolated CI 19 / 19 on the tag;
                     pin unchanged (6.7.10).
                     1.16.5 (folded at
                     cyrius 6.7.15, tag @ 9c67b33): THE BYTES / TEXT READ
                     PATH. A length of 0 is the only empty value; a
                     malformed chain (a bad page marker, an oversized or
                     negative page length, a link out of the file, a
                     chain looping back or running past its ref's length)
                     keeps the row's length and reads `PATRA_ERR_PAGE`; a
                     ref naming more bytes than the file can hold answers
                     length -1 and `PATRA_ERR_PAGE` (the user's decision,
                     2026-10-18). Through 1.16.4 each read as an empty
                     value — or, looping, the wrong bytes — with
                     `PATRA_OK` (the malformed chain since 1.12.8, the
                     oversized ref since 1.14.0). -1 is also the
                     getters' answer for a column of another type or a
                     cell outside the result set: the read's code tells
                     them apart (`PATRA_ERR_TYPE` / `PATRA_ERR_PARAM`
                     versus `PATRA_ERR_PAGE`) — size a buffer only from a
                     length >= 0 and check the read's return. (A
                     NEGATIVE stored length read `PATRA_ERR_PAGE` with
                     its getter giving the stored value, and
                     `patra_insert_row` accepted one with `PATRA_OK` —
                     both closed by 1.16.6, above.) Harnesses: `fuzz_bytes` plants its mutations on the
                     chain the row names (it took a heap pointer for a
                     page number and wrote past EOF since 1.12.8) and
                     asserts the reader's answer; the macOS failed-sync
                     row runs through the sync seam (macOS syncs
                     /dev/null); every scratch path comes from one helper,
                     tests/scratch_path.cyr (relative on Windows), and CI
                     refuses a bare `/tmp` literal. Suite 1,752 on Linux
                     x86_64 / aarch64, 1,675 on ecb / ach, 1,672 on cass;
                     isolated CI 19 / 19 on the tag. lib/patra.cyr
                     10,011 -> 10,033 lines; no public fn added or
                     removed; pin unchanged (6.7.10).
                     1.16.4 (folded at
                     cyrius 6.7.14, tag @ bbded00): its OWN suite runs on
                     Windows — per-target scratch paths (a drive-rooted
                     "/tmp/x" need not exist there) and the NUL device for
                     the failed-sync row; 1,565 / 1,565 on real cass with
                     cyrius 6.7.14's PE lseek fix. Tests only: `dist/` differs
                     in its version header alone; no API change; pin
                     unchanged (6.7.10).
                     1.16.3 (folded at
                     cyrius 6.7.13, tag @ b8722b7): on Windows a contended
                     `xflock` (-11, `LockFileEx` since cyrius 6.7.12) is
                     read as contended, so a contended `patra_open` defers
                     to WAL recovery instead of judging the header
                     unlocked; with cyrius 6.7.13's `xflock` re-lock fix
                     (a re-taken lock stays held) a second handle's writes
                     inside a transaction are no longer lost on Windows.
                     No API change; pin unchanged (6.7.10).
                     1.16.1 + 1.16.2 (folded at cyrius 6.7.11, tag @ 3937505):
                     `patra_open` before `patra_init` returns 0 instead
                     of faulting; `patra_initialized()`; failures are
                     REPORTED, not taken for success — a failed
                     `fdatasync` is `PATRA_ERR_IO` and sticky on the
                     handle until reopen, a refused allocation or write
                     fails the statement, a COMMIT that cannot be whole
                     and durable rolls back (`PATRA_ERR_FULL` /
                     `PATRA_ERR_IO`); headers verified after WAL recovery;
                     three lost-row paths and `CREATE TABLE t ()` fixed.
                     API: +6 public fns (`patra_initialized`,
                     `page_free_buf`, `tbl_chain_rows`, `tbl_unappend`,
                     `wal_mark_failed`, `wal_writes`), `tbl_scan_where`
                     9 -> 10 arguments (an overflow out-parameter); the
                     DB handle 88 -> 96 B, `patra_init` reserves 48 KB
                     more, nine thread-local slots (was six). 1.16.0
                     (6.7.6): WAL recovery before every statement. Bumped at
                     cyrius v5.6.20 from 1.5.5 to 1.6.0 for blob
                     support (sit consumer). Bumped again at v5.6.40
                     from 1.6.0 to 1.8.3 — INSERT OR IGNORE syntax
                     (1.7.0), STR-keyed B+ tree indexes (1.7.1), 4KB
                     page-slab allocator + word-at-a-time _memeq256 +
                     prepared statements (`patra_prepare` /
                     `patra_exec_prepared` / `patra_query_prepared` /
                     `patra_finalize`) (1.8.2), fmt/lint/doc cleanup
                     (1.8.3). **v5.7.9 BREAKING, minor bump
                     1.8.3 → 1.9.0**: renamed `fn json_build/6` →
                     `fn patra_json_build/6` to clear the silent
                     cross-module collision with
                     `lib/json.cyr::json_build/1` (the general pairs-
                     vec utility). cyrius v5.7.9's new duplicate-fn
                     warning surfaces this kind of collision at
                     registration. Consumer migration: rename
                     `json_build(buf, max, keys, vals, types, n)`
                     calls to `patra_json_build(...)`. The 7-arg
                     `json_build_lens` is unchanged. Bundled:
                     dist/patra.cyr.

  sigil (3.13.13)  — System-wide trust verification (SHA-256/512/SHAKE,
                     Ed25519, ECDSA + ECDH P-256 / P-384, X25519, HMAC,
                     HKDF, X.509 / PEM, TPM, AES-NI, SHA-NI). 3.13.8 +
                     3.13.9 (folded at cyrius 6.6.15): constant-time
                     ecdh_p256_* / ecdh_p384_*, ECDSA signing moved onto
                     the same constant-time engine (CYRIUS-2026-0023 — every field
                     op on the nonce path branched on secret data since
                     3.5.9), the signing nonce no longer left in dead
                     stack or xmm1, and the Windows trust helpers fail
                     closed instead of probing plantable rooted paths
                     (CYRIUS-2026-0024). 3.13.12 (folded at cyrius 6.7.10):
                     `hash_file_into`'s buffers are per-call stack locals,
                     so including sigil / tls / tls_native is warning-free
                     and the fold's static data falls 367,664 -> 105,520 B.
                     3.13.13 (folded at cyrius 6.7.16, tag @ 2ed419c): a
                     subprocess redirect never closes the std fd it just
                     made — `agnosys_run_capture[_timeout]` /
                     `agnosys_run_checked[_timeout]` skip the `dup2` and
                     the close when the source fd already IS the target,
                     so a caller started with a std fd closed gets its
                     capture (it was Err) and every checked run's child
                     keeps fds 1 and 2 — the class 6.7.15 fixed in
                     lib/process.cyr, lib/regression.cyr and lib/pam.cyr
                     (LF-6; field_notes/language/shell_runtime.cyml
                     `a_parent_with_a_std_fd_closed_hands_that_number_to_its_next_fd_v6715`).
                     Its new subprocess_closed_std_fds.tcyr (all eight
                     closed-fd masks on the four verbs) 50 / 50 on x86_64
                     Linux, ecb, ach, pi and qemu-aarch64 (18 failures on
                     3.13.12); isolated CI 17 / 17 on the tag; the diff
                     against 3.13.12's fold is the `# Version:` line and
                     the two fix hunks (19 + / 7 -); pin unchanged.
                     Bundled: dist/sigil.cyr. v2.9.0 added HKDF +
                     SHAKE-128/256; v2.9.1 consumers use AES-NI
                     cleanly after v5.5.21 fixed SSE m128 16-byte
                     alignment; v2.9.2 added SHA-NI CPUID probe +
                     dispatch entry point; v2.9.3 landed the SHA-NI
                     compress (drop-in replacement for software
                     SHA-256, ~80x throughput win on x86_64 SHA-NI
                     hosts; surfaced by sit v0.6.4's perf review).
                     Bumped 2.9.0 → 2.9.3 at cyrius v5.6.43.

  yukti (2.3.18) — Device abstraction layer for AGNOS. 2.3.18 (folded
                     at cyrius 6.7.16, tag @ 3efbba9): THE BUNDLE
                     COMPILES ALONE — sakshi and patra are `[deps].stdlib`
                     folds in yukti's manifest, so the bundle's
                     `# Requires` block now names them (16 modules) and
                     `include "lib/yukti.cyr"` alone builds on every
                     target; 2.3.17's left patra / sakshi names undefined
                     (`undefined variable 'PATRA_OK'`, 25
                     undefined-function warnings). patra is now compiled
                     into an agnos build too, unused there (+7,728 B
                     under DCE, +151,088 B without). The diff against
                     2.3.17's fold is the version line, 13 requires
                     lines and a comment; suite 845 / 845 (also
                     qemu-aarch64), isolated CI 17 / 17 on the tag; pin
                     unchanged. 2.3.17 (folded
                     at cyrius 6.7.10): no raw syscall left — the udev
                     monitor waits through the stdlib `sys_ppoll`, and
                     yukti no longer declares `SYS_PPOLL`.
                     Bundled: dist/yukti.cyr and dist/yukti-core.cyr
                     (kernel-safe core profile).

  mabda (4.2.0)   — GPU foundation (wgpu-native shim + native NVIDIA/nouveau backend, compute dispatch).
                     Bundled: dist/mabda.cyr. Note: 141KB+ bundle
                     requires input_buf >= 256KB (raised in v3.4.19).
                     v2.5.0 depends on the u64-keyed hashmap added
                     in cyrius v5.5.20 (map_u64_* family). 4.2.0 (folded
                     at cyrius 6.7.6, a minor): the 11 `F64_*` colour
                     constants are f64 `const` (they read 0 unless
                     `color_init()` had run).

  sankoch (2.8.9) — Lossless compression (LZ4, LZ4F, DEFLATE, zlib, gzip, zstd,
                     xz, zip; Brotli decode, and encode since 2.8.4).
                     2.8.4 - 2.8.7 (folded at cyrius 6.7.11): the Brotli
                     ENCODER (RFC 7932: `brotli_compress` /
                     `brotli_compress_bound` / `brotli_compress_level`,
                     ~71 KB on x86 when reached, @2.8.4); CRC-32 folds
                     with PCLMULQDQ on x86_64 (15–21x, bit-identical,
                     @2.8.5); the 2.8.x closeout @2.8.6 — two HIGHs (the
                     zip streaming writer's member offset, a doubly
                     created API lock), LZ4F and xz decode as strictly as
                     `lz4 -d` / liblzma, linked-block LZ4F frames decode;
                     @2.8.7 the encoder's overflow flag is `_bre_ovf` — as
                     `_bre_err` it was the SAME top-level global as
                     niyama's (cyrius accepts a same-value repeat), found
                     by 6.7.11's distlib owner-rule census.
                     Bundled: dist/sankoch.cyr. Bumped 2.0.1 → 2.0.3
                     at cyrius v5.6.35 (deflate-encoder fixes for
                     sit-tree-shaped inputs); bumped 2.0.3 → 2.1.0 at
                     cyrius v5.6.43 (DEFLATE compress perf — pre-
                     reversed dynamic Huffman codes, 8-byte word-
                     compare match extension, ring-buffer absolute-
                     position match-finder; wire-format byte-for-byte
                     identical, no API change). 2.7.6 (cyrius v6.4.79) is
                     a High/silent correctness fix: batch gzip/deflate
                     re-encoded up to 258 B at every 1 MiB block boundary,
                     so every container image over 1 MiB failed CRC-32.
                     Encoders now publish their reached offset — the
                     contract the STREAMING encoder always had. The suite
                     missed it for years because every deflate test used
                     input under 1 MiB.

  bayan (1.11.0)  — Data formats + big-int: json / toml / cyml / csv / yaml /
                     markdown / base64 / bigint (u256) / u128. CARVED out of the
                     cyrius stdlib at cyrius v6.1.25 (the 7 modules left
                     stdlib so bare-metal consumers don't drag data-format
                     code into kernel objects). FOLDED byte-identical into
                     cyrius lib/bayan.cyr (sandhi-pattern — NOT a git dep).
                     Public fns renamed bayan_* (json_parse → bayan_json_parse,
                     u256_add → bayan_u256_add, …) with legacy aliases for
                     the migration window. Opt-in: include "lib/bayan.cyr".
                     ganita (math: matrix/linalg/advanced) is the sibling
                     carve at cyrius v6.1.26. 1.5.14 (folded at cyrius
                     6.7.10): `bayan_f64_parse` hands every non-NaN /
                     Inf input to the stdlib's `f64_parse_n` (its own
                     parser tiers gone, bundle −18.7 KB, bit-identical).
                     1.6.0 (folded at cyrius 6.7.12 at the user's
                     request, the user's tag @ dd8f9c7):
                     `bayan_cyml_parse` takes 0.39x 1.5.14's time — it
                     hops newline to newline instead of testing every
                     byte for an `[[entries]]` marker, twice; the private
                     `_cyml_is_entries` is gone; no public fn added,
                     removed or answering differently (a 4.6M-parse
                     differential: 0 differences). Pin 6.7.9 -> 6.7.11,
                     the floor stays 6.7.9.
                     1.7.0 – 1.9.0 (folded at cyrius 6.7.13, tag @ 0c626c6;
                     answers change — re-check):
                     ⚠ 1.7.0 csv reads and writes RFC 4180 as Python's
                     `csv` does — a written record ends in CRLF (it was
                     LF), a trailing empty field is a field (`a,` is two
                     fields), a line's own CR / LF no longer lands in its
                     last field, a field holding CR is quoted;
                     `bayan_json_build` writes valid JSON by each pair's
                     kind (it quoted every value) and ⚠ a flat pair is 24 B
                     (`JSON_PAIR_SIZE` 16 -> 24, `bayan_json_pair_new_kind`)
                     — code that allocated a pair itself must change; cyml
                     markers / `---` are whole lines wherever they fall
                     (and a CRLF document parses). 1.7.1 the csv writers
                     return 0 on a refused allocation. 1.8.0 yaml quoted
                     scalars come back DECODED (YAML 1.2 escapes, `''` in
                     single quotes; a bad escape is refused with its
                     offset) — drop any decoding of bayan's output, or it
                     decodes twice. 1.9.0 `bayan_markdown_*`, a CommonMark
                     0.31.2 parser + HTML renderer, GFM extensions by flag
                     (raw HTML is text); a hand-written include list needs
                     `lib/unicode/categories.cyr` and
                     `lib/unicode/casefold.cyr` before `lib/bayan.cyr` (a
                     program that never calls it builds without them,
                     with three undefined-function warnings). +31 public
                     fns; pin 6.7.12, the floor stays 6.7.9.

  ganita (1.2.15) — Linear algebra + advanced math: matrix (ganita_mat_*),
                     linalg (LU/det/inv/Cholesky/QR/least-squares/eigen/SVD),
                     and the advanced math fns (transcendental + fibonacci/
                     binomial) SPLIT from stdlib lib/math.cyr. CARVED at
                     cyrius v6.1.26 (closes the data/math stdlib carve with
                     bayan). FOLDED byte-identical into cyrius lib/ganita.cyr.
                     Renamed ganita_* with legacy aliases. Keep stdlib `math`
                     in scope (f64-exp/ln polyfills + F64 constants). Opt-in:
                     include "lib/ganita.cyr".
                     1.2.13 (folded at cyrius 6.6.16, tag @ 486b8f5):
                     ⚠ `ganita_mat_svd` is a NEW algorithm (one-sided
                     Jacobi on a column- and row-pivoted QR — Drmač–
                     Veselić, LAPACK dgejsv / dgesvj), so every SVD's
                     bits change; it no longer refuses finite matrices
                     that have an SVD (hisab 3.3.3's filings) and is
                     faster from 8×8 up. RETURN CODES CHANGE: SVD
                     non-convergence is −3 (was −1); NaN / infinite input
                     is refused across linalg (−2); `rank` passes −2 / −3
                     through; `eigen_sym`'s allocation failure is −1 (was
                     −2). `pseudo_inv` gives no NaN when σ underflows and
                     `pseudo_inv` / `condition` are right when σ₁
                     overflows. No public signature changed.

  sandhi (1.13.6) — Service boundary: HTTP client + server, TLS policy, routing,
                     discovery (field_notes/language/stdlib_format.cyml
                     `sandhi_service_layer_dep`). 1.10.7 (folded at cyrius
                     6.6.16, tag @ a13398a; 1.10.5 / 1.10.6 never folded
                     alone): concurrent requests no longer run under EACH
                     OTHER's TLS policy (the hook moved from two
                     process-wide globals into the per-call request
                     context — measured 162–167 of 180 unpolicied requests
                     accepted certificates their own verification
                     rejects); a policied open skips the session cache; a
                     whole-request budget against Slowloris
                     (`sandhi_server_options_request_ms`, default 60 s,
                     408 on plaintext); a disconnecting client no longer
                     kills a sandhi server on macOS (SIGPIPE). Fixed: a
                     short body vs Content-Length is SANDHI_ERR_PROTOCOL;
                     `Connection: close` bodies were cut at their first NUL;
                     HEAD / 204 / 304 complete at the header block; HTTPS
                     routing no longer grows the global heap per request.
                     Added: stream idle turns, `sandhi_router_dispatch_c_a`.
                     1.10.9 (folded at cyrius 6.7.6): the pooled serve
                     loops key on CHAN_BLOCKING (language/features.cyml
                     `chan_blocking_capability_v6616`), so macOS / Windows
                     pools run on worker threads; a Windows stop flag stops
                     every serve loop.
                     1.10.10 – 1.13.1 (folded at cyrius 6.7.13, tag @
                     bc7ef13; no API change for a caller): a failed serve
                     entry point says why and a failed bind / listen no
                     longer leaks the listen socket (1.10.10); the pooled
                     servers check their worker threads started (1.11.x);
                     the `loop` / `break` sweep (1.12.x, no behaviour
                     change); a client connection pool may be shared
                     across threads — each pool owns a mutex (1.13.0; one
                     HTTP/2 connection used by several threads at once
                     was still not safe until 1.13.3, below); the h2 ALPN
                     offer and `_auto`'s 0-RTT opt-in / credential digest
                     live in each request's context (1.13.1). Pin 6.7.12.
                     1.13.2 – 1.13.3 (folded at cyrius 6.7.16, tag @
                     cd5d541; no public verb added or removed, 5 private
                     helpers; the Requires block and dist/sandhi.deps
                     unchanged): a server's request framing reads
                     `Content-Length` / `Transfer-Encoding` in place —
                     nothing kept on the global heap per request (16 B a
                     body request before; sit's filing) — and a
                     heap-exhausted copy no longer reads as an absent
                     header in the CL+TE / TE guards (1.13.2); ONE h2
                     CONNECTION MAY BE SHARED BY SEVERAL THREADS — each
                     owns a lib/sync.cyr mutex held for one exchange, so
                     its requests are serialised
                     (`SANDHI_H2_CONN_STRUCT_SIZE` 80 -> 88: code that
                     sized a connection itself must change); the pool's
                     h2 close waits for an exchange in flight, and a
                     later exchange on the stale pointer fails as
                     `SANDHI_ERR_PROTOCOL` (it could reach a connection
                     that had reused the descriptor); the h2 / HPACK
                     constructors fail closed on an allocation failure
                     (1.13.3). Pin 6.7.15.

TO USE ANY OF THESE — they ship WITH the toolchain, so there is nothing
to fetch. Name the leaf in the stdlib list and include it:

    [deps]
    stdlib = ["std", "sigil"]

    include "lib/sigil.cyr"

Then: cyrius deps

The git-dep form is still supported and is what you want for a library
that is NOT folded into the toolchain:

    [deps.mylib]
    git = "https://github.com/MacCracken/mylib.git"
    tag = "1.0.0"
    modules = ["dist/mylib.cyr"]

Do NOT point a [deps.NAME] at a folded sibling to "get a newer version" —
that puts two copies of the same symbols in one compilation unit. To move
a fold forward, fix it UPSTREAM, version-bump, regenerate the dist
(including every sub-profile), and re-vendor.
